From e55557d92713c1a5009d3e869e710194e7614627 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Patrik Eriksson <patrik.m.eriksson@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 12:11:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] cdng: write unpacked 16-bit and 8-bit payloads straight
 from the frame, in one write()

Why: for Clear HDR 16-bit (and 1-byte 8-bit) frames the writer's row pass is a plain
memcpy of rows whose stride equals the row length, so every DNG cost a second full copy
of the frame (16.6 MB at 4K) through a 491,520-byte scratch and 34 write() calls, where
cinepi-raw writes the assembled frame once. The kb's research (working/dng-chain, R3 and
R8) counts three full copies per frame in Core against cinepi-raw's two, about 2.5 GB/s
of memory traffic at 25 fps, on a board whose libcamera cycle the kb measured losing
instructions per cycle while recording.

What: writeRawPayload writes the payload from the frame itself when writeDngRowBytes would
copy the row unchanged (unpacked 16-bit, unpacked 1-byte 8-bit): one write() when the rows
are contiguous, one iovec per row (writev, IOV_MAX rows per call) when the stride is padded.
Packed, PiSP-compressed and 10/12-bit unpacked layouts keep the scratch path. Two knobs for
the A/B: APERTAR_DNG_PAYLOAD_COPY=1 forces the scratch path for every frame (the old
behaviour); APERTAR_DNG_WRITE_ROWS (default 64, the old 64-row chunk) sets the rows per
write() on the scratch path.

Tested: tools/dng_payload_write_test.sh on the host: six layouts (4K 16-bit contiguous and
padded stride, 8-bit mono, 12-bit unpacked and CSI2-packed controls, a small odd stride)
written with the direct path, the forced scratch path and APERTAR_DNG_WRITE_ROWS=512 are
byte-identical; strace counts 1 write() for the 4K 16-bit frame instead of 36. Not run on
the Pi.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01NEFc1zNNwfkFFgzHEzb7wV
